// Joystick — the controller test app. A labelled map of the controls
// @8bitscript/input exposes, with a lamp on each one that flashes when
// that control is pressed, so a human sitting in front of a machine can
// see at a glance whether its input layer answers at all.
//
// This is the program to run first on a machine whose input layer is new
// or suspect. It is deliberately the whole of what the portable surface
// can say and nothing else: six controls, two counters and a line of
// capability facts. If a lamp never lights, that machine's layer does not
// answer that control — which is a fact about the layer, not about this
// program.
//
// ---- what a lamp actually means -------------------------------------------
//
// **A lamp is a press that was seen, not a control that is held.** That
// distinction is the whole reason this file is shaped the way it is, and
// it is worth being blunt about because the obvious reading of a
// controller map is the wrong one here.
//
// @8bitscript/input is edge-triggered by design: `up()` is true on the one
// frame the press begins and false for every frame it is held (see
// packages/input/AGENTS.md — a level-triggered answer races a menu
// highlight across a bar in a third of a second, so the surface does not
// offer one). There is no `held()` or `down()` on the surface at all. The
// only level-triggered call on it is `pointer()`, which this program does
// not use.
//
// So "light it up while it is pressed" is not a question this API can
// answer, and pretending otherwise would be a screen that implies more
// than the code does. What this program does instead is latch: the edge
// sets a per-lamp countdown to LAMP_FRAMES, every frame decrements it, and
// the lamp is lit while the countdown is running. A single press therefore
// flashes for about a quarter of a second — long enough to see, and
// honestly labelled on screen as a flash rather than drawn as a held
// state. Hold a direction down and the lamp flashes once and goes out,
// because that is exactly what the API reported.
//
// ---- why reverse video, and not colour ------------------------------------
//
// Three of the nine targets have no per-cell colour at all (the PET has no
// colour RAM, the Atari 8-bit's GR.0 has none, and the NES's colour lives
// in a 2x2 attribute block its text layer does not touch — see
// packages/ui/AGENTS.md). `text.setReverse` works on all nine, so the lamp
// *is* reverse video and colour only ever rides on top of it. The colour
// calls below are made unconditionally rather than behind
// `if (Video.COLOR_PER_CELL)`: `text.setColor` is a deliberately empty
// function on those three machines and the compiler deletes the call, so
// they pay nothing for it and this file never has to ask.
//
// Every label carries its own leading and trailing space — " UP " rather
// than "UP" — so a lit lamp is a solid bar with the word knocked out of
// it rather than two inverted letters, which at 8x8 is the difference
// between reading across the room and squinting.
//
// ---- reading the idle screen ----------------------------------------------
//
// `--screenshot` cannot press anything, so a headless capture only ever
// shows the idle state. That state is still worth capturing, because it
// asserts two things:
//
//   - **SEEN must read 00000.** It counts every edge this program has ever
//     been handed and never decays. A machine left completely alone whose
//     counter is climbing is reporting phantom presses — which is a real
//     bug shape on this hardware, not a hypothetical: a layer that
//     edge-detects a *composed* direction (SHIFT plus a cursor key) sees
//     every release's stray frame of bare cursor key as a fresh press.
//     packages/input/AGENTS.md records that measured under xpet, and
//     records that the C64, C128, VIC-20 and MEGA65 layers still compose
//     their cursor directions the old way.
//   - **FRAME must be climbing.** It is bumped once per trip round the
//     loop, so a frozen program and an idle one look different — and two
//     captures at different `--frames` counts show it even in stills,
//     which a moving spinner would not. On a machine where waitFrame()
//     is not really pacing, it also says so: 600 frames of capture that
//     leave FRAME in the tens of thousands means the wait returned
//     instantly, which is exactly the shape of the PET bug where a
//     skipped input.poll() leaves the retrace flag stuck set.
//
// Neither of those is evidence that input *works*. Only a human with a
// controller in their hands can say that.
//
// One thing an idle run already found: **the MEGA65 reads SEEN 00001, and
// the press it counts is CONFIRM.** Measured under xmega65, 2026-09-12, at
// 300, 600, 1200 and 2400 frames — the count is 1 at every one of them, so
// it happens once at start-up and never repeats.
//
// The cause was measured too, not guessed, because this layer's confirm is
// an OR of two different things (RETURN, or joystick 2's fire) and the two
// want opposite fixes. A scratch build read CIA1 itself on the first frame,
// before input.poll(): column 0 of the matrix and then port A, the same two
// reads @8bitscript/mega65/input makes. **RETURN read down and the stick
// read all-clear** (`002` and `000`), and the identical probe on a C64 and
// a C128 — same CIA, same addresses, same autostart — read `000` and `000`
// with SEEN at 00000. So it is the emulator's own `RUN` still holding
// RETURN when the program reaches its first poll on this machine and not on
// those two, not a stuck port bit and not this layer's joystick read.
//
// Which makes it a fact about the surface rather than about the MEGA65:
// every layer starts its edge detector with `before = 0`, so **anything
// already held when the first `poll()` runs reads as a press that just
// began.** A real machine does the same thing to a player holding fire as
// the program loads. Priming the snapshot with one read in `begin()` would
// close it everywhere at once; nothing here works around it, because the
// workaround belongs in @8bitscript/input and not in a program.
//
// ---- the PORTS line, and why it is on screen ------------------------------
//
// The bottom line reports `#fact(input.keyboard)`, `#fact(input.joysticks)`
// and `#fact(input.pads)` for the machine this build was made for, as
// KEY/JOY/PAD. It is there to separate two failures that look identical
// from the lamps alone:
//
//   - **the machine has nothing to press** — a PET reads KEY 1 JOY 0
//     PAD 0, so a dark LEFT lamp there means nobody pressed the cursor
//     keys, and there is no stick to blame; and
//   - **the layer does not read what the machine has** — a Commander X16
//     reads KEY 1 JOY 0 PAD 2 and still lights nothing ever, because
//     @8bitscript/cx16/input answers false to every direction, confirm
//     and cancel: its SNES pads (KERNAL joystick_get, $FF56) and its
//     keyboard (GETIN, $FFE4) are not read yet, and its header says so.
//     A dark map there is the layer's coverage, not the machine's.
//
// These are facts, so all three numbers are settled while compiling and
// the line costs nothing to work out at run time.
//
// ---- what it costs -------------------------------------------------------
//
// Measured 2026-09-12, stock hardware for every target, program bytes then
// RAM for variables:
//
//   pet    2298 / 62   of a stock 4K 2001's 3071   — the tightest fit
//   vic20  2414 / 70   of an unexpanded 3583
//   c128   2412 / 63      mega65 2436 / 63      c64    2571 / 64
//   atari8 2633 / 73      cx16   3258 / 156     nes    40976 / 94 (fixed ROM)
//   web     117 bytes of constant data / 41
//
// The two that matter are the first two: this app has to fit the smallest
// machine it claims to run on or it is not a test app for that machine,
// and it clears the 4K PET by 773 bytes and the unexpanded VIC-20 by
// 1169. The X16 is the heaviest of the eight real machines, and
// `8bs build cx16 --size` says why: not this program, but VERA
// addressing under it — `locate` alone is 363 bytes, the three
// `locateTextMap` copies another 153, and the screen inset and its
// `cellOn` clamp 123 more, because a 76-column map is not a shift away
// from a cell index the way a 40-column one is. Its KERNAL mouse
// (`mouse_begin`, 48 bytes) is compiled in because `#fact(input.mouse)`
// is true there and the hardware really has one; this program never
// calls the pointer half of the surface and pays only that.
import { screen } from "@8bitscript/screen";
import { text, TextColor } from "@8bitscript/text";
import { input } from "@8bitscript/input";
import { Input } from "@8bitscript/system";

// This machine's row width, so cell = y * ROW + x. A bare const name is
// the only thing besides a literal that a global initializer may be, so
// everything derived from it is worked out at run time in computeLayout()
// rather than written as a const expression here.
const ROW: usmallint = text.COLUMNS;

// The map is drawn 22 columns wide and 18 rows tall, because 22 is the
// VIC-20's whole screen — the narrowest of the nine — and 23 rows is its
// whole height, which is also what the 18 has to clear (a blank row
// between the cross's arms is what makes it read as a cross rather than
// a paragraph, and that spacing is the whole of the height budget).
// Everything wider (40 on most, 76 on an X16, 80 on a MEGA65 or a PET
// 8032) gets the same map centred in it, so the app looks deliberate
// rather than jammed into a corner on the big machines.
const MAP_WIDTH: utinyint = 22;
const MAP_TOP: utinyint = 1; // one blank row above; not worth bytes to centre vertically

// How long one press stays lit, in frames. 15 is a quarter of a second at
// 60Hz and three tenths at 50: long enough to catch out of the corner of
// an eye, short enough that two deliberate taps read as two flashes. It
// has not been checked against a fast repeat by hand — nothing in a
// headless run can press a key — so if a human finds a double-tap fusing
// into one flash, this is the number to lower.
const LAMP_FRAMES: utinyint = 15;

// The six lamps, in the order they are indexed below. Named as consts so
// the update and the paint cannot drift apart.
const UP: utinyint = 0;
const DOWN: utinyint = 1;
const LEFT: utinyint = 2;
const RIGHT: utinyint = 3;
const CONFIRM: utinyint = 4;
const CANCEL: utinyint = 5;
const LAMPS: utinyint = 6;

// Where each lamp's label starts, worked out once in computeLayout().
// Six separate `let`s rather than an `array<usmallint, 6>` because a
// two-byte array element cannot be *written* yet — only read, the way
// 2048's POW2 table is read — so the array that would obviously hold
// these is not one the compiler will store into today.
let cellUp: usmallint = 0;
let cellDown: usmallint = 0;
let cellLeft: usmallint = 0;
let cellRight: usmallint = 0;
let cellConfirm: usmallint = 0;
let cellCancel: usmallint = 0;

// Frames left on each lamp's flash, and what is actually on screen for it
// (0 dark, 1 lit) so a lamp that did not change is not redrawn. That
// dirty check is not cosmetic: the NES delivers cell writes in vertical
// blank 112 bytes at a time, and repainting six labels every frame would
// spend most of that budget on writes that change nothing.
let lampTimer: array<utinyint, 6>;
let lampShown: array<utinyint, 6>;

// Every edge this program has ever been handed, and what is on screen for
// it. Never decays — see the header on why 00000 is the assertion an idle
// screenshot makes.
let seen: usmallint = 0;
let seenShown: usmallint = 65535; // not a count: forces the first paint
let seenCell: usmallint = 0;

// Trips round the main loop, wrapping at 65535 — the liveness signal.
// Repainted every frame without a dirty check, because it changes every
// frame by definition: five cells a frame, well inside even the NES's
// 112-byte vertical-blank budget.
//
// This started as a reverse-video block walking an 8-cell track, which is
// a better "it is alive" signal at a glance, and it was replaced after a
// screenshot: **on the Commander X16 a cell that has been printed in
// reverse cannot be printed back to normal.** @8bitscript/cx16/text
// implements reverse as a swap of VERA's attribute nibbles, and its
// non-reverse path keeps the cell's existing background nibble on purpose
// (`(attr & 0xF0) | currentColor`) so a print does not undo
// `screen.setBackground` — but a reverse print has already written the
// text colour *into* that background nibble, so the cell stays filled.
// Measured under x16emu, 2026-09-12: the walking block left its whole
// 8-cell track lit behind it as one solid bar. On the other eight targets
// reverse is a flip of the character code and reverses cleanly, which is
// why this only showed up on one machine. The lamps below still use
// reverse, because reverse is the only highlight all nine share; the X16
// is the one machine where a lamp that lit could not go dark again, and
// it is also the one machine where no lamp can ever light, so the two
// cancel out until its input layer answers. There is no portable
// workaround and this program does not attempt one.
let frames: usmallint = 0;
let frameCell: usmallint = 0;

// The map's top-left cell. computeLayout() works it out and drawMap()
// reads it, rather than each deriving it: the multiply and the centring
// are ~40 bytes and there is no reason to link two copies.
let mapOrigin: usmallint = 0;

// Every cell address on the map, from this machine's own row width. Run
// time, not compile time: `const` folds a literal or another const's bare
// name and nothing else, so there is no way to write these as consts.
function computeLayout(): void {
    // Centre the 22-wide map in however many columns this machine has.
    let indent: utinyint = 0;
    if (text.COLUMNS > MAP_WIDTH) {
        indent = (text.COLUMNS - MAP_WIDTH) >> 1;
    }
    let origin: usmallint = MAP_TOP;
    origin = origin * ROW + indent;
    mapOrigin = origin;

    // The cross: UP on its own row, LEFT and RIGHT either side of the row
    // below it, DOWN under the gap between them. The columns are chosen so
    // " UP " (4 wide) and " DOWN " (6 wide) both centre on the 7-cell gap
    // that " LEFT " (6) and " RIGHT " (7) leave between them.
    cellUp = origin + 2 * ROW + 9;
    cellLeft = origin + 4 * ROW + 1;
    cellRight = origin + 4 * ROW + 14;
    cellDown = origin + 6 * ROW + 8;

    // The two decisions, on their own row under the cross, because they
    // are not directions and should not read as part of the d-pad.
    cellConfirm = origin + 8 * ROW + 1;
    cellCancel = origin + 8 * ROW + 12;

    seenCell = origin + 10 * ROW + 6; // after "SEEN "
    frameCell = origin + 11 * ROW + 7; // after "FRAME "
    return;
}

// The static half of the screen, drawn once before the loop starts.
// Everything the loop touches after this is the six labels and the two
// five-digit counters — nothing else on screen ever changes.
function drawMap(): void {
    let origin: usmallint = mapOrigin;

    text.print(origin + 3, "CONTROLLER TEST");
    text.print(origin + 10 * ROW + 1, "SEEN");
    text.print(origin + 11 * ROW + 1, "FRAME");
    // The one line that keeps the map honest: the lamps are edges, not
    // held state, and a person reading a controller map will assume held
    // unless told. See this file's header.
    text.print(origin + 13 * ROW + 1, "LAMP FLASHES ON");
    text.print(origin + 14 * ROW + 1, "EACH PRESS SEEN");

    // What the machine itself declares it has — see this file's header.
    // `Input.KEYBOARD` is a bool and printNumber takes a number, so it is
    // widened here; both sides of the `if` are compile-time constants, so
    // this is one literal by the time the backend sees it.
    let keyboards: utinyint = 0;
    if (Input.KEYBOARD) {
        keyboards = 1;
    }
    text.print(origin + 16 * ROW + 1, "KEY");
    text.printNumber(origin + 16 * ROW + 5, keyboards, 1);
    text.print(origin + 16 * ROW + 7, "JOY");
    text.printNumber(origin + 16 * ROW + 11, Input.JOYSTICKS, 1);
    text.print(origin + 16 * ROW + 13, "PAD");
    text.printNumber(origin + 16 * ROW + 17, Input.PADS, 1);
    return;
}

// One frame of one lamp's state: an edge relights it and is counted, and
// anything already lit burns down toward dark.
function tick(lamp: utinyint, began: bool): void {
    if (began) {
        lampTimer[lamp] = LAMP_FRAMES;
        seen++;
        return;
    }
    if (lampTimer[lamp] != 0) {
        lampTimer[lamp]--;
    }
    return;
}

// Draw a lamp, but only if what it should look like is not already what
// is on screen. The label carries its own padding spaces so the lit form
// is a solid bar rather than inverted letters.
function paint(lamp: utinyint, cell: usmallint, label: string): void {
    let want: utinyint = 0;
    if (lampTimer[lamp] != 0) {
        want = 1;
    }
    if (want == lampShown[lamp]) {
        return;
    }
    lampShown[lamp] = want;
    if (want != 0) {
        text.setColor(TextColor.YELLOW);
        text.setReverse(true);
    } else {
        text.setColor(TextColor.WHITE);
    }
    text.print(cell, label);
    text.setReverse(false);
    return;
}

export function main(): void {
    screen.blank();
    computeLayout();
    drawMap();
    input.begin();

    // Force every lamp to paint once, dark, so the map is complete before
    // the first press rather than appearing a control at a time.
    for (let i: utinyint = 0; i < LAMPS; i++) {
        lampShown[i] = 255; // not a lamp state: guarantees the first paint
    }

    while (true) {
        waitFrame();
        // Exactly one poll() per waitFrame(), never twice: every layer's
        // header says so, and on the PET reading the matrix is also what
        // clears the vertical-retrace flag, so a skipped poll stops the
        // next waitFrame() from pacing at all.
        input.poll();

        tick(UP, input.up());
        tick(DOWN, input.down());
        tick(LEFT, input.left());
        tick(RIGHT, input.right());
        tick(CONFIRM, input.confirm());
        tick(CANCEL, input.cancel());

        paint(UP, cellUp, " UP ");
        paint(DOWN, cellDown, " DOWN ");
        paint(LEFT, cellLeft, " LEFT ");
        paint(RIGHT, cellRight, " RIGHT ");
        paint(CONFIRM, cellConfirm, " CONFIRM ");
        paint(CANCEL, cellCancel, " CANCEL ");

        if (seen != seenShown) {
            seenShown = seen;
            text.printNumber(seenCell, seen, 5);
        }

        // The liveness signal, every frame. Nothing else on screen moves
        // while the machine is idle, so this is what separates a program
        // that is waiting from one that has stopped.
        frames++;
        text.printNumber(frameCell, frames, 5);
    }
}
